              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": ". Note, however, that the order in which the initializers are invoked depends on whether they implement Spring’s "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Ordered"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " interface or are annotated with Spring’s "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "@Order"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " annotation or the standard "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "@Priority"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " annotation. The following example shows how one class can extend another and use both its own initializer and the superclass’s initializer:"
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
